1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
From sideshows to scientific exploration,

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:07,000
aerial advertising to the edge of space,

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:11,140
now the colorful and remarkable history of balloons

4
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:12,940
on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
The story of the balloon is filled with unbridled romance

6
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:37,400
and uncanny resilience.

7
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:40,920
How else can you explain why an unsteerable lighter-than-air

8
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:44,620
bag with no brakes, a basket, and no way to propel itself

9
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:49,480
is so compelling to watch and even more popular to fly?

10
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:54,900
Ballooning is a mystery to even balloonists themselves.

11
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,900
Balloons are a symbol of a new balloon.

12
00:00:57,901 --> 00:01:04,020
Balloons are a symbol of freedom, whether they're high-altitude, long-duration gas balloons

13
00:01:04,021 --> 00:01:07,540
or low-altitude, short-duration hot air balloons.

14
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:10,180
They defy the grip of gravity.

15
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:11,960
They are unrestrained.

16
00:01:13,820 --> 00:01:17,020
Balloons are just air particles.

17
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:23,520
They do absolutely nothing that the weather doesn't make them do.

18
00:01:23,521 --> 00:01:27,520
For centuries, the balloon has provided those pioneers

19
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:30,120
brave enough to travel where the weather takes them

20
00:01:30,220 --> 00:01:33,160
with the possibilities to exploit their dreams.

21
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:37,060
The balloon is the free spirit of air travel.

22
00:01:37,160 --> 00:01:39,140
You go where the wind tells you to go.

23
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:41,680
And so therefore, the control you have over the balloon

24
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:43,480
is very minimal.

25
00:01:43,580 --> 00:01:45,280
It's going to go wherever the wind is going.

26
00:01:45,380 --> 00:01:48,880
Balloons are the most natural, I think,

27
00:01:48,980 --> 00:01:51,240
way to fly in the air.

28
00:01:51,241 --> 00:01:55,140
And flying in the air is just a wonderful, wonderful thing

29
00:01:55,240 --> 00:01:55,940
to do.

30
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:01,520
On a cold morning in March of 1999,

31
00:02:01,620 --> 00:02:04,520
Team Breitling was busy preparing their balloon

32
00:02:04,620 --> 00:02:08,860
for one of aviation's oldest dreams and ultimate challenges,

33
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:13,000
to fly nonstop around the world by balloon.

34
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:16,500
For more than a decade, a handful of long-distance balloonists

35
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:21,240
had tried and failed to circumnavigate the globe.

36
00:02:21,241 --> 00:02:24,580
forced down by the inevitable pull of Earth's gravity,

37
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:27,540
fickle winds, and material failures.

38
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:30,880
But the balloon has always been a public relations bonanza,

39
00:02:30,980 --> 00:02:33,020
and the round-the-world attempts galvanized

40
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:34,780
the public's attention.

41
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:37,620
A balloon is kind of a universal fun symbol,

42
00:02:37,720 --> 00:02:39,720
a universal adventure symbol.

43
00:02:39,820 --> 00:02:43,400
And everyone just loves a balloon.

44
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:45,400
The adventure became a rare test

45
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:49,940
of raw nerves, scientific ingenuity, and lucky breaks.

46
00:02:49,941 --> 00:02:54,220
This time, Breitling pilots Brian Jones and Bertrand Piccard

47
00:02:54,221 --> 00:02:57,980
would fly a balloon that was as tall as the Leaning Tower of Pisa

48
00:02:57,981 --> 00:03:00,480
and weighed as much as a fighter plane.

49
00:03:02,820 --> 00:03:07,060
What's interesting is that other than its size and the materials used to make it,

50
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:11,460
the basic design of their balloon has remained fundamentally unchanged

51
00:03:11,461 --> 00:03:13,680
for more than 200 years.

52
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:18,080
Man has dreamed of flying through the air for thousands of years,

53
00:03:18,081 --> 00:03:26,820
But it wasn't until 1783 that the Montgolfier brothers demonstrated the first balloon to rise under its own power, hot air.

54
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:33,340
Since hot air is lighter than cold air, hot air held in an enclosed bag or envelope will rise.

55
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:36,060
Their first balloons were made out of paper.

56
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:40,440
They were not particularly the right shape because they were just learning.

57
00:03:41,020 --> 00:03:45,620
But they were just paper envelopes, and they would hold them over a fire,

58
00:03:45,621 --> 00:03:50,040
and they would fill with hot air, and when they let go of the paper, they'd fly away.

59
00:03:50,460 --> 00:03:56,240
Glue wasn't very available in those days, so when they had to transport the balloon,

60
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:01,220
they couldn't just crumple it up in a bag as we do today with flexible fabrics.

61
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:07,140
They had to assemble the balloon, and they did it with buttons and buttonholes.

62
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:12,220
The first manned flight was made by Francois Palatra de Rosier,

63
00:04:12,221 --> 00:04:15,900
who flew the Montgolfier's paper and cloth hot air balloon

64
00:04:15,901 --> 00:04:19,680
for almost 25 minutes before a fire forced him down.

65
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:21,360
They did catch some fire.

66
00:04:22,220 --> 00:04:28,660
Part of the equipment was long poles with a sponge on the end of it.

67
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:31,920
And when a spark would go up and start a fire in the envelope,

68
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:35,700
they'd dump the sponge in the bucket of water and put the fire out.

69
00:04:35,701 --> 00:04:40,160
In December of 1783, French chemist J.A. Charles

70
00:04:40,161 --> 00:04:43,660
made the first manned flight in a balloon filled with hydrogen gas,

71
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:48,060
a newly discovered source of lift that is seven times lighter than air.

72
00:04:49,140 --> 00:04:57,200
Charles was using silk because the hydrogen molecule is so much smaller than an air molecule,

73
00:04:57,540 --> 00:05:02,740
and they would coat the silk with tree sap to make it non-porous.

74
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:07,320
By 1785, in an effort to extend the length of flight,

75
00:05:07,321 --> 00:05:11,660
de Rosier tried to combine both sources of lift into one balloon.

76
00:05:11,661 --> 00:05:16,560
Using an open fire, hot air rose up the cylinder to warm hydrogen,

77
00:05:16,561 --> 00:05:18,560
which made the gas even lighter.

78
00:05:18,561 --> 00:05:21,560
Unfortunately, hydrogen is highly flammable,

79
00:05:21,561 --> 00:05:25,040
and the Rosier balloon blew up shortly after takeoff.

80
00:05:25,041 --> 00:05:30,140
The first man to fly in a balloon was also the first man to die in a balloon.

81
00:05:30,141 --> 00:05:33,880
From then on, balloonists did not mix hydrogen and hot air.

82
00:05:33,881 --> 00:05:38,440
they used only hydrogen gas and later non-flammable helium for lift.

83
00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:41,540
As soon as the gas balloon came out, the hot air balloon was obsolete.

84
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:43,720
It could only make short flights.

85
00:05:44,460 --> 00:05:47,940
It seemed to be hazardous, taking fire aloft and everything.

86
00:05:48,360 --> 00:05:52,700
So the hot air balloon just disappeared, and it was gas ballooning

87
00:05:52,701 --> 00:05:56,720
until the turn of the century.

88
00:05:57,360 --> 00:06:02,240
Ironically, 214 years after man's first hot air balloon flight,

89
00:06:02,241 --> 00:06:07,720
It was a Rosier-style balloon that would be the first to fly nonstop around the world.

90
00:06:08,260 --> 00:06:14,980
Because balloons sink at night when temperatures drop and gas cools and rise again during the day when the sun expands the gas,

91
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:20,100
a constant source of lift is needed to keep the balloon at an altitude with favorable winds.

92
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:31,720
In a Rosier balloon, a large pocket of non-flammable helium enclosed above is warmed by a separate cone-shaped bag of hot air generated by burning propane below.

93
00:06:32,240 --> 00:06:35,940
The challenge for pilots is to negotiate changing altitudes

94
00:06:36,040 --> 00:06:40,420
while keeping the helium and propane from running out.

95
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:42,620
For balloonists, the ultimate dream

96
00:06:42,720 --> 00:06:46,480
became a reality on March 21, 1999,

97
00:06:46,580 --> 00:06:50,320
when the Breitling Orbiter 3 completed the 29,000 mile

98
00:06:50,420 --> 00:06:53,660
journey around the world in just under 20 days

99
00:06:53,760 --> 00:06:57,360
at an average speed of 60 miles per hour.

100
00:06:57,460 --> 00:06:59,840
Everybody wanted to be first, and a lot of people

101
00:06:59,841 --> 00:07:03,280
have quit because they said they wanted to be first

102
00:07:03,380 --> 00:07:05,080
or not do it.

103
00:07:05,180 --> 00:07:08,620
But you know somebody is going to say, 20 days,

104
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:11,380
I can do better than that, and they're going to try.

105
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:13,480
Long before the balloon was utilized

106
00:07:13,580 --> 00:07:16,060
to satisfy man's competitive nature,

107
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:18,800
it was employed as an instrument of war.

108
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:22,200
As early as 1794, the French used tethered balloons

109
00:07:22,300 --> 00:07:24,660
to scout the movements of enemy troops.

110
00:07:24,760 --> 00:07:27,760
Never before had soldiers been able to observe battle

111
00:07:27,761 --> 00:07:30,500
from such a vantage point.

112
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:33,960
Tethered balloons were also used in the American Civil War

113
00:07:34,060 --> 00:07:37,040
to map battlegrounds and direct artillery.

114
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:39,700
At altitudes of 500 feet, observers

115
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:42,480
could see almost 25 miles.

116
00:07:42,580 --> 00:07:46,720
In 1861, Thaddeus Lowe, an early aeronaut for the Union,

117
00:07:46,820 --> 00:07:49,080
made a historic and rare free flight

118
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:51,760
in a balloon over Confederate positions.

119
00:07:51,860 --> 00:07:54,880
He was actually able to return to Union territory

120
00:07:54,881 --> 00:07:57,380
on a fortuitous high-altitude current.

121
00:07:57,980 --> 00:08:01,820
In 1870, balloons were the only link to the outside world

122
00:08:01,821 --> 00:08:03,460
during the Franco-Prussian War.

123
00:08:04,020 --> 00:08:06,100
With all ground communication cut off,

124
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:09,440
66 balloons carried more than 100 people

125
00:08:09,441 --> 00:08:11,720
and 2.5 million letters out of Paris.

126
00:08:13,460 --> 00:08:14,940
Here's World War I.

127
00:08:15,320 --> 00:08:17,080
No gas generator in sight.

128
00:08:18,020 --> 00:08:20,940
The hydrogen is forced under high pressure into cylinders,

129
00:08:21,460 --> 00:08:24,160
which can then be stored or carried almost anywhere.

130
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:36,188
While powered steerable balloons with interior skeletons or rigid airships ruled the air in 1917, the ground-based round balloon was replaced by a more stable sausage

131
00:08:36,189 --> 00:08:38,640
shape suitable for higher altitudes.

132
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:49,120
Armed with more sophisticated communication systems, balloonists were released by winches to elevations of 6,000 feet or more to relay enemy positions.

133
00:08:49,121 --> 00:08:52,120
While these balloons were highly effective at spying,

134
00:08:52,220 --> 00:08:55,320
few positions were as frightening as that of the balloon's pilot

135
00:08:55,420 --> 00:08:58,120
in the face of enemy gunfire.

136
00:08:58,220 --> 00:09:01,960
If gunfire didn't get him, fire from the highly flammable

137
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:04,160
hydrogen gas balloon would.

138
00:09:04,260 --> 00:09:06,880
So under attack, the balloonist's only recourse

139
00:09:06,980 --> 00:09:09,880
was to bail out and parachute to safety.

140
00:09:09,980 --> 00:09:13,080
By the end of the war, the manned balloons were downed,

141
00:09:13,180 --> 00:09:15,980
but not out.

142
00:09:16,080 --> 00:09:17,820
At the beginning of World War II,

143
00:09:17,821 --> 00:09:20,820
the Japanese hoped to set huge forest fires in the United States

144
00:09:20,821 --> 00:09:23,820
by launching balloons carrying incendiary bombs.

145
00:09:23,821 --> 00:09:26,820
Made of paper or silk, the Japanese launched

146
00:09:26,821 --> 00:09:30,820
an estimated 9,000 bomb-carrying balloons.

147
00:09:30,821 --> 00:09:36,820
Only 285 reached the United States and caused minimal damage.

148
00:09:36,821 --> 00:09:39,820
Perhaps the most successful use of balloons in war

149
00:09:39,821 --> 00:09:42,820
was the unmanned barrage balloon.

150
00:09:42,821 --> 00:09:46,820
Anchored by steel cables, these 70,000 cubic foot

151
00:09:46,821 --> 00:09:51,620
rubberized cotton balloons formed an impenetrable aerial picket

152
00:09:51,720 --> 00:09:52,420
line.

153
00:09:54,920 --> 00:09:57,960
Instead of the cable remaining taut and possibly breaking or

154
00:09:58,060 --> 00:10:00,840
slipping off the wing of the plane, it pulls free.

155
00:10:00,940 --> 00:10:04,640
And a parachute system pulls a bomb right onto the plane.

156
00:10:04,740 --> 00:10:08,940
Barrage balloons were capable of flying up to 20,000 feet,

157
00:10:09,040 --> 00:10:12,620
forcing enemy aircraft to drop their bombs well out of range

158
00:10:12,720 --> 00:10:15,220
of American targets at sea.

159
00:10:15,221 --> 00:10:18,220
It took Team Breitling more than $18 million

160
00:10:18,221 --> 00:10:20,220
to circumnavigate the globe.

161
00:10:20,221 --> 00:10:22,220
Balloons will continue on Modern Marvels.

162
00:10:26,220 --> 00:10:29,220
We now return to Balloons on Modern Marvels.

163
00:10:31,220 --> 00:10:34,220
Balloonists risked their lives for science

164
00:10:34,221 --> 00:10:36,220
well before they risked their lives in war.

165
00:10:36,221 --> 00:10:40,220
Scientists of all kinds were among the first professionals

166
00:10:40,221 --> 00:10:42,220
to exploit the potential of the balloon,

167
00:10:42,221 --> 00:10:45,220
in part to dispel popular myths of flight.

168
00:10:45,320 --> 00:10:47,620
People were very superstitious in those days,

169
00:10:47,720 --> 00:10:51,220
and they thought if you left the ground, you'd die.

170
00:10:51,320 --> 00:10:55,260
So they sent up a rooster, a sheep, and a duck.

171
00:10:55,360 --> 00:10:59,440
And they flew, they landed, they ran over to the balloon,

172
00:10:59,540 --> 00:11:02,340
they were all in one piece, so they said it's okay.

173
00:11:02,440 --> 00:11:04,880
After that, the insatiable curiosity

174
00:11:04,980 --> 00:11:07,180
of the scientific aeronauts changed the way

175
00:11:07,280 --> 00:11:09,340
we viewed the world forever.

176
00:11:09,440 --> 00:11:11,180
They all have their own agendas,

177
00:11:11,181 --> 00:11:13,680
and they all had their own goals and objectives.

178
00:11:13,780 --> 00:11:17,220
But the three things they have in common

179
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:21,420
was smarts, guts, and iron will.

180
00:11:21,520 --> 00:11:24,900
In 1862, meteorologist James Glacier

181
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,700
and Henry Coxwell made several balloon flights

182
00:11:27,800 --> 00:11:29,860
above 20,000 feet.

183
00:11:29,960 --> 00:11:32,340
Among their discoveries was the jet stream,

184
00:11:32,440 --> 00:11:34,940
a high-speed current of air that circles the Earth

185
00:11:35,040 --> 00:11:36,940
between layers of atmosphere.

186
00:11:37,040 --> 00:11:40,640
On July 11, 1897, Swedish aeronaut

187
00:11:40,641 --> 00:11:48,380
Solomon Andre and two colleagues attempted to fly a 170,000 cubic foot silk balloon to the North Pole

188
00:11:48,381 --> 00:11:52,040
in an effort to learn more about the climate in which they lived.

189
00:11:52,900 --> 00:11:59,060
In a message received by carrier pigeon four days later, all was well, but the crew was never heard

190
00:11:59,061 --> 00:12:05,073
from again. Their remains and these pictures were not discovered until 1930.

191
00:12:05,074 --> 00:12:06,260
That same year,

192
00:12:06,261 --> 00:12:11,540
Swiss physicist Auguste Piccard began working on a pressurized gondola,

193
00:12:11,541 --> 00:12:17,100
an enclosed system designed to reuse its own supply of air at high altitude.

194
00:12:17,101 --> 00:12:22,140
This system was the forerunner to the one used by his grandson Bertrand Piccard,

195
00:12:22,141 --> 00:12:25,520
the first balloonist to fly around the world.

196
00:12:25,521 --> 00:12:30,660
In 1931, Auguste Piccard was interested in studying cosmic rays.

197
00:12:30,661 --> 00:12:34,220
To do that, he not only needed his own pressurized air supply,

198
00:12:34,221 --> 00:12:36,600
but he needed a balloon, a big one.

199
00:12:36,940 --> 00:12:40,200
The envelope was made of static-free rubberized cotton

200
00:12:40,201 --> 00:12:42,300
and weighed 1,600 pounds.

201
00:12:42,301 --> 00:12:46,400
On two occasions, the 500,000 cubic foot balloon

202
00:12:46,401 --> 00:12:48,880
hoisted Picard in his aluminum gondola

203
00:12:48,881 --> 00:12:52,920
above 50,000 feet, almost 10 miles into the sky.

204
00:12:53,580 --> 00:12:55,900
I believe the New York Times had an editorial in the 30s

205
00:12:55,901 --> 00:12:58,700
that described the high-altitude pioneers of the 30s

206
00:12:58,701 --> 00:13:00,140
as Vikings of the air.

207
00:13:00,141 --> 00:13:05,420
And that's very much the motivation for some of these guys.

208
00:13:05,421 --> 00:13:08,820
They wanted to be out there on the leading edge and be the first to see these things.

209
00:13:08,821 --> 00:13:15,260
With bigger balloons and pressurized capsules, the sky soon became a limitless research laboratory.

210
00:13:15,261 --> 00:13:20,600
One of the most exciting and neglected chapters in aviation history was about to begin.

211
00:13:20,601 --> 00:13:26,900
The piece of hardware that was really crucial that made the 50s high-altitude balloon projects possible

212
00:13:26,901 --> 00:13:30,920
was the invention of new materials for balloon envelopes,

213
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:32,900
specifically plastic and polyethylene.

214
00:13:33,360 --> 00:13:36,840
It seems quite flimsy, but it is really quite strong.

215
00:13:39,560 --> 00:13:41,620
And it will hold air or helium.

216
00:13:45,020 --> 00:13:47,100
The new plastic balloon material,

217
00:13:47,300 --> 00:13:49,360
which was as thin as a dry cleaning bag,

218
00:13:49,361 --> 00:13:53,100
was exposed to temperatures of 100 degrees below zero,

219
00:13:53,320 --> 00:13:55,460
as well as the direct rays of the sun.

220
00:13:55,461 --> 00:14:00,460
This material represented a quantum leap in scientific balloon design.

221
00:14:00,560 --> 00:14:06,400
Once you got into polyethylene, you had incredibly strong, incredibly light material,

222
00:14:06,500 --> 00:14:13,920
and you could extrude it in long gores of material that you could make a huge balloon

223
00:14:14,020 --> 00:14:17,520
capable of holding millions of cubic feet of helium.

224
00:14:17,620 --> 00:14:23,460
Larger, lighter, stronger balloons meant heavier scientific payloads and higher flights.

225
00:14:23,461 --> 00:14:27,460
And higher flights meant man was on his way into space.

226
00:14:27,461 --> 00:14:30,460
They absolutely were the first step into space.

227
00:14:30,461 --> 00:14:33,460
The polyethylene balloon came along at the right time,

228
00:14:33,461 --> 00:14:37,460
because it was a very inexpensive way to gather the knowledge

229
00:14:37,461 --> 00:14:39,460
and the data that we needed.

230
00:14:39,461 --> 00:14:42,460
Manai was an Air Force project, and the goal of the project

231
00:14:42,461 --> 00:14:45,460
from the very beginning was to raise a human being

232
00:14:45,461 --> 00:14:49,460
above 100,000 feet, 20 miles above the surface of the Earth,

233
00:14:49,461 --> 00:14:52,460
leave him there, bring him back down safely.

234
00:14:52,461 --> 00:14:53,460
safely.

235
00:14:53,560 --> 00:14:55,260
But which man would they send?

236
00:14:55,360 --> 00:14:58,160
And who had the right stuff before we

237
00:14:58,260 --> 00:15:00,360
knew what the right stuff was?

238
00:15:00,460 --> 00:15:01,900
I was a fighter pilot, and I was

239
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,200
an experimental test pilot, and I volunteered

240
00:15:04,300 --> 00:15:07,900
for every interesting project that came along.

241
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,280
The man chosen for the first man-high

242
00:15:10,380 --> 00:15:13,340
stratospheric balloon flight was Air Force test pilot

243
00:15:13,440 --> 00:15:14,540
Joe Kittinger.

244
00:15:14,640 --> 00:15:18,180
When I went from a supersonic F-100 to a subsonic balloon,

245
00:15:18,280 --> 00:15:19,880
it was quite a transition.

246
00:15:19,881 --> 00:15:24,320
It's a game between you and the elements on how you steer a balloon,

247
00:15:24,420 --> 00:15:27,220
but you can't go any faster than what the wind is,

248
00:15:27,320 --> 00:15:29,620
and you go the same direction that the wind is going in.

249
00:15:29,720 --> 00:15:33,720
He was a brilliant man, a very gutsy man,

250
00:15:33,820 --> 00:15:38,140
and he was willing to take on assignments that no one else wanted to do.

251
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:42,900
The mani capsule was really a testament to what we call

252
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,840
sort of bootleg, shirt sleeves research.

253
00:15:45,841 --> 00:15:50,640
This thing was essentially an aluminum can with a door

254
00:15:50,740 --> 00:15:51,680
on the front.

255
00:15:51,780 --> 00:15:55,620
It really was held together with bailing wire,

256
00:15:55,720 --> 00:16:00,380
and there was cardboard to keep the sun from shining

257
00:16:00,480 --> 00:16:02,320
in the pilot's eyes.

258
00:16:02,420 --> 00:16:06,720
It was remarkably low tech.

259
00:16:06,820 --> 00:16:09,860
The gondola, which measured three feet by seven feet

260
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:12,160
and weighed more than 2,000 pounds,

261
00:16:12,260 --> 00:16:14,200
was minute compared to the balloon,

262
00:16:14,201 --> 00:16:17,600
which had a capacity of 2 million cubic feet.

263
00:16:17,700 --> 00:16:21,300
On takeoff, the balloon was about 35 foot across.

264
00:16:21,400 --> 00:16:24,280
But as a balloon goes aloft and the pressure decreases,

265
00:16:24,380 --> 00:16:27,520
the balloon expands so that when it gets to altitude,

266
00:16:27,620 --> 00:16:33,180
it's over 200 foot across and looks just like an onion.

267
00:16:33,280 --> 00:16:35,260
The trickiest part of a balloon ascension,

268
00:16:35,360 --> 00:16:36,720
a high-altitude balloon ascension,

269
00:16:36,820 --> 00:16:38,160
is when you hit the jet stream, when

270
00:16:38,260 --> 00:16:40,100
you hit that big gust of wind, which

271
00:16:40,200 --> 00:16:42,220
might be 100 mile an hour wind.

272
00:16:42,221 --> 00:16:48,780
And what it tends to do to a huge balloon envelope is as if big fingers are grabbing it and smearing it around.

273
00:16:49,040 --> 00:16:51,040
That's the part that you really cross your fingers on.

274
00:16:51,820 --> 00:16:58,880
When I went up on man high, I had about a 40-mile-an-hour wind at 40,000 feet.

275
00:16:58,881 --> 00:17:04,600
And I looked up at the balloon, and it was actually concave where the wind was hitting it.

276
00:17:05,020 --> 00:17:08,660
And it was fluttering, but it didn't break, of course.

277
00:17:08,661 --> 00:17:15,660
With the jet stream well beneath him, Kittinger and his balloon finally reached maximum altitude.

278
00:17:15,661 --> 00:17:18,660
The altimeter read 97,000 feet.

279
00:17:18,661 --> 00:17:25,660
He was above 99.9% of the Earth's atmosphere and was the first man in space.

280
00:17:25,661 --> 00:17:30,660
One of my radios quit working, so I had to use Mars code, which slowed down the transmission data.

281
00:17:30,661 --> 00:17:33,500
But when I ended up at 97,000 feet,

282
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:36,800
I had a tenth of a liter of liquid oxygen left,

283
00:17:36,900 --> 00:17:38,600
which is not very much.

284
00:17:38,700 --> 00:17:41,540
One of the valves had been installed backwards.

285
00:17:41,640 --> 00:17:45,480
So it was a challenge to get up and to get down

286
00:17:45,580 --> 00:17:47,420
before I ran out of oxygen.

287
00:17:47,520 --> 00:17:49,940
After valving off excess helium,

288
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:52,380
Kittinger was able to land safely.

289
00:17:52,480 --> 00:17:55,020
He and a handful of other brave scientists

290
00:17:55,120 --> 00:17:57,680
continued to make stratospheric balloon flights,

291
00:17:57,780 --> 00:18:00,160
researching everything from cosmic rays

292
00:18:00,161 --> 00:18:03,300
rays and the ozone to the limits of human tolerance.

293
00:18:04,380 --> 00:18:09,940
The biggest reason why a balloon was chosen for a lot of these types of projects was simply

294
00:18:09,941 --> 00:18:10,740
the expense.

295
00:18:11,340 --> 00:18:14,980
We didn't have a lot of vehicles that could get us up above 100,000 feet.

296
00:18:15,760 --> 00:18:18,980
Kittinger made a total of five balloon flights into space.

297
00:18:19,580 --> 00:18:25,500
In 1960, as head of Project Excelsior, he piloted a 3 million cubic foot balloon with

298
00:18:25,501 --> 00:18:30,080
an open gondola to a record shattering 102,000 feet.

299
00:18:30,160 --> 00:18:39,740
This time the balloon rises more than 14 miles for a jump that will test equipment and procedures for emergency re-entry from outer space.

300
00:18:40,240 --> 00:18:42,420
It's a long way down.

301
00:18:43,080 --> 00:18:52,260
A camera strapped to Captain Kittinger records these views as he drops 12 miles in three minutes before the shield finally breaks his fall.

302
00:18:52,261 --> 00:18:58,700
Not only was Kittinger the first man to break the sound barrier with only his body,

303
00:18:59,200 --> 00:19:04,340
but he proved beyond reasonable doubt that a pilot could survive a high-altitude bailout.

304
00:19:05,280 --> 00:19:11,760
It wasn't long after that that President Kennedy committed the nation to ambitious and bold goals in space.

305
00:19:12,220 --> 00:19:16,440
We choose to go to the moon in this decade and do the other things,

306
00:19:16,820 --> 00:19:20,080
not because they are easy, but because they are hard.

307
00:19:20,081 --> 00:19:23,400
The announcement all but broke the shoestring budgets

308
00:19:23,401 --> 00:19:26,460
of the remaining high-altitude balloon programs.

309
00:19:27,360 --> 00:19:30,740
Stargazer really was the last gasp of the Air Force.

310
00:19:30,920 --> 00:19:34,680
Occurred in 1962, and it was an astronomical research flight.

311
00:19:35,040 --> 00:19:40,220
The idea was to raise a powerful telescope up into the stratosphere

312
00:19:40,221 --> 00:19:45,400
and essentially do astronomy above the haze and the murk

313
00:19:45,401 --> 00:19:47,600
and the distortion that we have in our atmosphere.

314
00:19:47,601 --> 00:19:50,440
It was the first chance we really had to look at the moon,

315
00:19:50,540 --> 00:19:55,040
to look at Mars, without the distortion.

316
00:19:55,140 --> 00:19:57,980
After the initial launch, Stargazer's balloon,

317
00:19:58,080 --> 00:20:01,480
which was 3 million cubic feet and carried a payload

318
00:20:01,580 --> 00:20:07,160
of more than 6,500 pounds, never made it off the ground again.

319
00:20:07,260 --> 00:20:09,380
So we had two balloon fairs in a row,

320
00:20:09,480 --> 00:20:11,700
and the Air Force said, Kettinger,

321
00:20:11,800 --> 00:20:14,060
why don't you do something else?

322
00:20:14,160 --> 00:20:17,600
So I left, and I went into Vietnam as a result.

323
00:20:17,601 --> 00:20:18,600
result of that.

324
00:20:18,601 --> 00:20:22,900
It's different, but it's very pretty out here.

325
00:20:22,901 --> 00:20:28,800
If the central technoscientific moment of the 20th century was the lunar landing, then

326
00:20:28,801 --> 00:20:34,040
we have to say that the maiden voyage for that flight really goes all the way back to

327
00:20:34,041 --> 00:20:40,980
the balloonists in the 50s, flying in a vehicle that was very little changed from the 18th

328
00:20:40,981 --> 00:20:41,980
century.

329
00:20:41,981 --> 00:20:44,980
The legacy of the high-altitude balloon programs really was a NASA in the space program.

330
00:20:44,981 --> 00:20:49,980
We would not certainly have gotten to the moon as quickly

331
00:20:50,080 --> 00:20:53,020
or as easily without them.

332
00:20:53,120 --> 00:20:55,620
The highest manned balloon flight ever recorded

333
00:20:55,720 --> 00:21:00,360
was 123,500 feet in 1966.

334
00:21:00,460 --> 00:21:02,800
Balloons will return on Modern Marvels.

335
00:21:05,940 --> 00:21:11,240
We now return to balloons on Modern Marvels.

336
00:21:11,340 --> 00:21:13,480
Ever since the 19th century, ballooning

337
00:21:13,481 --> 00:21:17,240
belonged primarily to the scientists, the military,

338
00:21:17,241 --> 00:21:22,280
and the European elite, because lifting gas was so expensive.

339
00:21:22,281 --> 00:21:26,600
In Europe, non-flammable helium was 10 times more expensive

340
00:21:26,601 --> 00:21:28,840
than highly flammable hydrogen.

341
00:21:28,841 --> 00:21:31,240
And in the US, nervous gas companies

342
00:21:31,241 --> 00:21:33,880
were reluctant to sell balloonists hydrogen,

343
00:21:33,881 --> 00:21:36,440
forcing them to use the more expensive helium.

344
00:21:36,441 --> 00:21:40,120
But helium and hydrogen balloons had other drawbacks.

345
00:21:40,121 --> 00:21:41,720
Both gases are heavy.

346
00:21:41,721 --> 00:21:44,420
They may be lighter than air once they fill the balloon,

347
00:21:44,560 --> 00:21:48,860
but each steel cylinder of fuel under pressure weighs about 100 pounds,

348
00:21:48,861 --> 00:21:52,260
which prohibits balloonists from taking a fuel supply on board.

349
00:21:52,880 --> 00:21:59,200
Also, the early rubberized cotton gas balloons were made of heavy material that was easily damaged.

350
00:21:59,980 --> 00:22:04,980
They had to be reworked and inspected inside and out for pinholes and everything else after your flight.

351
00:22:05,620 --> 00:22:09,780
Ed Yost changed all that by taking the hydrogen and helium out of the balloon

352
00:22:09,781 --> 00:22:12,920
and putting propane-heated hot air back in,

353
00:22:13,400 --> 00:22:17,100
more than 150 years after the Montgolfier brothers.

354
00:22:17,340 --> 00:22:19,160
He knew scientific balloons.

355
00:22:19,740 --> 00:22:21,060
He knew gas balloons.

356
00:22:21,520 --> 00:22:24,040
So his first balloon, his first hot air balloon,

357
00:22:24,100 --> 00:22:25,340
was built like a gas balloon.

358
00:22:25,880 --> 00:22:31,800
In 1955, Yost built a 27,000-cubic-foot polyethylene prototype.

359
00:22:32,420 --> 00:22:35,920
The first one I made, I used white unleaded gas

360
00:22:35,921 --> 00:22:38,860
and the burners from Plumber's fire pots

361
00:22:38,861 --> 00:22:42,860
and ganged them together and got the thing inflated in the air on a string,

362
00:22:43,100 --> 00:22:45,960
actually a nylon line, and got some pictures.

363
00:22:46,060 --> 00:22:49,100
And I took the pictures back to Washington and showed the Office of Naval Research

364
00:22:49,101 --> 00:22:57,080
that you could get a longer flight duration and carry your fire and fuel on board.

365
00:22:57,081 --> 00:23:01,980
The Navy commissioned Yost to develop a new balloon that would carry one man

366
00:23:01,981 --> 00:23:04,120
and enough fuel for several hours.

367
00:23:04,121 --> 00:23:09,000
It had to be small, lightweight, reusable, and inflate quickly.

368
00:23:09,780 --> 00:23:17,040
Pretty soon he had the teardrop shape that we see today that every balloon manufacturer in the world uses.

369
00:23:17,640 --> 00:23:29,080
And there are stories about how that shape was devised by computer, by submerging rubber envelopes in water, all kinds of magic things.

370
00:23:29,081 --> 00:23:32,360
But what it was was just a good old cut and try.

371
00:23:32,361 --> 00:23:37,080
Just do it, improve it, and keep improving it until you've got what works.

372
00:23:37,820 --> 00:23:40,940
And the materials, the fabric, he went through the same thing.

373
00:23:41,020 --> 00:23:42,860
He went through all the fabrics that were available.

374
00:23:43,800 --> 00:23:50,380
Dacron, nylon, Orlon, plastics arrived at nylon.

375
00:23:51,280 --> 00:23:53,100
Through a series of experiments.

376
00:23:54,120 --> 00:23:55,760
The burners were a problem.

377
00:23:57,920 --> 00:24:00,100
I had 14 flame mounts on the fire.

378
00:24:00,101 --> 00:24:05,100
And when I had the whole burner exploded near,

379
00:24:05,101 --> 00:24:08,100
I hit the ground pretty hard several times.

380
00:24:08,101 --> 00:24:12,100
I had a doctor in Sioux Falls named Dr. Frost.

381
00:24:12,101 --> 00:24:15,100
Every time he saw me coming, he said,

382
00:24:15,101 --> 00:24:18,100
Yost, he said he'd worn out three bodies already.

383
00:24:18,101 --> 00:24:23,100
He was a very brave man.

384
00:24:23,101 --> 00:24:26,100
I think he was as brave as among Gauphiers originally.

385
00:24:26,101 --> 00:24:28,100
He didn't know it was going to happen.

386
00:24:28,101 --> 00:24:32,100
In October of 1960, Yost made the first free flight

387
00:24:32,200 --> 00:24:34,200
in a modern hot air balloon.

388
00:24:34,300 --> 00:24:36,500
Equipped with a portable propane heater,

389
00:24:36,600 --> 00:24:39,840
he flew for 25 minutes and landed three miles

390
00:24:39,940 --> 00:24:41,140
from where he started.

391
00:24:41,240 --> 00:24:43,640
More importantly, the use of propane,

392
00:24:43,740 --> 00:24:46,560
which was both inexpensive and readily available,

393
00:24:46,660 --> 00:24:49,960
put ballooning in the hands of everyday adventurers.

394
00:24:50,060 --> 00:24:53,300
A balloon flight fueled by propane cost about $20,

395
00:24:53,400 --> 00:24:57,000
as compared to $3,000 a flight for helium.

396
00:24:57,001 --> 00:25:00,700
The thing that makes the hot air balloon a sport today

397
00:25:00,800 --> 00:25:02,600
is the fact that Ed Yost figured out

398
00:25:02,700 --> 00:25:05,740
how to do it cheap and portable.

399
00:25:05,840 --> 00:25:09,240
He figured out the right materials to use,

400
00:25:09,340 --> 00:25:15,660
the right fuel to use, and invented a sport.

401
00:25:15,760 --> 00:25:22,300
I don't think Ed Yost intended the hot air balloon to be a sport.

402
00:25:22,400 --> 00:25:25,960
I think he intended to be exactly what

403
00:25:25,961 --> 00:25:32,960
exactly what it was at the time he was fulfilling a military contract by inventing a military

404
00:25:32,961 --> 00:25:33,960
tool.

405
00:25:33,961 --> 00:25:35,960
It all turned out good.

406
00:25:35,961 --> 00:25:41,960
And, you know, not too many years after that, I got a lot of letters from Czechoslovakia

407
00:25:41,961 --> 00:25:42,960
and stuff.

408
00:25:42,961 --> 00:25:46,960
I sent them sketches and plans and stuff.

409
00:25:46,961 --> 00:25:52,960
And when that group from East Germany first made their own hot air balloon escape, I said,

410
00:25:52,961 --> 00:25:54,100
that it's all worthwhile.

411
00:25:56,100 --> 00:25:56,860
Oh, goodness.

412
00:25:57,500 --> 00:26:01,060
While the Navy eventually lost interest in hot air balloons,

413
00:26:01,500 --> 00:26:03,700
Yost was just getting warmed up.

414
00:26:04,040 --> 00:26:05,500
You don't make much of an impression

415
00:26:05,501 --> 00:26:08,940
with this machine crossing the United States

416
00:26:08,941 --> 00:26:10,420
and especially South Dakota.

417
00:26:10,980 --> 00:26:13,580
So if you really want the world to look at this thing,

418
00:26:13,640 --> 00:26:15,280
you have to do something that people look at.

419
00:26:15,840 --> 00:26:19,820
So Ed Yost and Auguste Picard's nephew, Don Picard,

420
00:26:19,821 --> 00:26:24,180
decided to fly a 56,000 cubic foot hot air balloon

421
00:26:24,181 --> 00:26:27,480
across the English Channel to demonstrate its capabilities.

422
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:31,460
To get clearance to fly this monster across the English Channel,

423
00:26:31,640 --> 00:26:34,320
we had to go to the Department of Transport in London,

424
00:26:34,321 --> 00:26:37,440
and they didn't know a damn thing about balloons,

425
00:26:37,680 --> 00:26:40,480
especially hot air balloons.

426
00:26:40,580 --> 00:26:43,280
Nobody even heard of one with carried fuel on board.

427
00:26:43,680 --> 00:26:47,900
In April of 1963, Yost and Picard took off from England

428
00:26:47,901 --> 00:26:51,540
and were immediately forced up to an unfamiliar altitude

429
00:26:51,541 --> 00:26:53,660
in search of more favorable winds.

430
00:26:54,140 --> 00:26:57,140
We stayed at 13,500 feet, and I didn't even know

431
00:26:57,141 --> 00:27:00,780
if the balloon would fly that high or the burners would operate,

432
00:27:00,860 --> 00:27:01,560
but they did.

433
00:27:01,980 --> 00:27:05,020
The flight lasted three hours, 17 minutes,

434
00:27:05,120 --> 00:27:07,140
and covered a distance of 50 miles.

435
00:27:07,540 --> 00:27:11,700
And whether he knew it or not, Ed Yost, the propane pioneer,

436
00:27:12,020 --> 00:27:15,120
had just put modern hot air ballooning on the map.

437
00:27:15,121 --> 00:27:18,560
The first aeronaut to cross the English Channel in a gas

438
00:27:18,620 --> 00:27:23,360
balloon was Frenchman Jean-Pierre Blanchard in 1785.

439
00:27:23,420 --> 00:27:27,040
Balloons will return on Modern Marvels.

440
00:27:27,100 --> 00:27:30,640
The oldest international gas balloon race began in Paris

441
00:27:30,700 --> 00:27:32,340
in 1906.

442
00:27:32,400 --> 00:27:35,780
The race was conceived and named for James Gordon Bennett,

443
00:27:35,840 --> 00:27:38,740
an eccentric millionaire and newspaper publisher.

444
00:27:38,820 --> 00:27:43,140
The first race featured 16 balloons from seven countries.

445
00:27:43,141 --> 00:27:46,740
The Gordon Bennett race was a great international challenge.

446
00:27:46,741 --> 00:27:48,380
These balloons are expensive.

447
00:27:48,381 --> 00:27:50,140
It's an expensive sport.

448
00:27:50,141 --> 00:27:53,580
A good friend of mine has got the perfect way to train

449
00:27:53,581 --> 00:27:55,380
for gas ballooning.

450
00:27:55,381 --> 00:27:59,380
You get into the shower and turn the water on ice cold

451
00:27:59,381 --> 00:28:01,100
and get completely soaked.

452
00:28:01,101 --> 00:28:03,500
And then you take all the $100 bills you can get

453
00:28:03,501 --> 00:28:04,900
and stuff them down the drain.

454
00:28:04,901 --> 00:28:08,540
And that's the way you train for flying gas balloons,

455
00:28:08,541 --> 00:28:10,140
because it's always cold.

456
00:28:10,141 --> 00:28:11,700
It frequently rains.

457
00:28:11,701 --> 00:28:14,900
And it's a rather expensive pastime,

458
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:16,280
but an awful lot of fun.

459
00:28:16,380 --> 00:28:19,280
To this day, the only rule in the Gordon Bennett race

460
00:28:19,380 --> 00:28:22,980
is that each competitor must fly the same size balloon,

461
00:28:23,080 --> 00:28:24,880
35,000 cubic feet.

462
00:28:24,980 --> 00:28:28,680
And whoever flies the farthest in any direction wins.

463
00:28:28,780 --> 00:28:33,460
Joe Kittinger has won the race three times in the Rosie O'Grady,

464
00:28:33,560 --> 00:28:35,420
but it wasn't easy.

465
00:28:35,520 --> 00:28:38,500
We ended up landing at night about 4 o'clock

466
00:28:38,501 --> 00:28:41,540
at about 4 o'clock on a desert island called the Guardian

467
00:28:41,541 --> 00:28:42,640
Angel Island.

468
00:28:42,641 --> 00:28:44,940
And there's no habitation on the island.

469
00:28:44,941 --> 00:28:47,440
And it has the highest percentage of rattlesnakes

470
00:28:47,441 --> 00:28:49,120
of any place in the world.

471
00:28:49,121 --> 00:28:52,140
But the great thing about it was we won the race.

472
00:28:52,141 --> 00:28:54,120
Racing is not the only challenge

473
00:28:54,121 --> 00:28:56,020
for competitive balloonists.

474
00:28:56,021 --> 00:28:59,560
There are world records in 15 different size categories

475
00:28:59,561 --> 00:29:01,960
for distance, duration, and altitude.

476
00:29:01,961 --> 00:29:04,060
Ed Yost holds many of them.

477
00:29:04,061 --> 00:29:05,620
Hey, I've been doing this for 50 years.

478
00:29:05,621 --> 00:29:08,200
I know what the hell I'm doing, I think.

479
00:29:08,201 --> 00:29:10,900
Ed Yost is the Babe Ruth of balloons.

480
00:29:11,320 --> 00:29:12,980
There's nothing he hasn't done.

481
00:29:13,520 --> 00:29:19,020
In October of 1976, Yost launched a 60,000 cubic foot helium balloon

482
00:29:19,021 --> 00:29:23,600
made of neoprene-coated nylon in a solo attempt to cross the Atlantic.

483
00:29:24,600 --> 00:29:26,840
He had no sponsors. He did it with his own money.

484
00:29:27,780 --> 00:29:31,020
He built the entire balloon himself.

485
00:29:31,021 --> 00:29:32,580
He built the envelope himself.

486
00:29:32,800 --> 00:29:35,300
He built the car underneath it himself.

487
00:29:35,301 --> 00:29:40,300
The car was a sailboat, so that if he had to go down in the water, he would be in a boat.

488
00:29:40,840 --> 00:29:46,660
The only thing that failed during the whole flight was his alarm clock, which he bought in a drugstore.

489
00:29:47,420 --> 00:29:53,080
And the alarm clock was to wake him up, of course, so he could sleep in small portions.

490
00:29:53,780 --> 00:29:58,100
And the alarm clock broke, so he repaired the alarm clock while he was in flight.

491
00:29:58,700 --> 00:30:04,640
My objective was to get all the world records, and I made 2,475 miles on a great circle.

492
00:30:04,641 --> 00:30:07,580
on this very small balloon that's farther than Los Angeles

493
00:30:07,581 --> 00:30:08,980
and New York City.

494
00:30:08,981 --> 00:30:12,920
And I was near four and a half days alone.

495
00:30:12,921 --> 00:30:14,180
Nobody to fight with.

496
00:30:14,181 --> 00:30:17,460
Yost landed 200 miles east of the Azores

497
00:30:17,461 --> 00:30:20,660
and set distance and duration records for that size balloon

498
00:30:20,661 --> 00:30:22,600
that still stand today.

499
00:30:22,601 --> 00:30:24,260
Everybody said, why don't you do it again?

500
00:30:24,261 --> 00:30:26,400
I said, well, I did one, and that's enough.

501
00:30:26,401 --> 00:30:29,340
I didn't cross the ocean, but if I had crossed the ocean,

502
00:30:29,341 --> 00:30:33,840
I'd have lost a lot of business because that was a challenge.

503
00:30:33,841 --> 00:30:35,800
So then I started getting telephone calls.

504
00:30:36,300 --> 00:30:40,300
One of the first calls Yost got was from Max Anderson and Ben Abruzzo,

505
00:30:40,580 --> 00:30:43,400
a couple of businessmen turned hot air balloonists

506
00:30:43,401 --> 00:30:46,540
who wanted to try and cross the Atlantic by gas balloon.

507
00:30:47,120 --> 00:30:48,740
There was just one problem.

508
00:30:49,140 --> 00:30:50,160
I trained them to fly.

509
00:30:50,560 --> 00:30:52,620
I went to Amarillo, Texas, and they came over,

510
00:30:52,800 --> 00:30:55,220
and I took plastic balloons and taught them to fly.

511
00:30:55,221 --> 00:30:59,360
In September of 1977, Anderson and Abruzzo launched

512
00:30:59,361 --> 00:31:03,580
a 101,000 cubic foot Yost-built balloon from Massachusetts.

513
00:31:03,840 --> 00:31:07,140
The trajectory on Double Eagle One was horrible.

514
00:31:07,141 --> 00:31:11,280
They went clear up in the Arctic and damn near froze to death in that operation.

515
00:31:11,281 --> 00:31:13,840
Severe winds blew them off course.

516
00:31:13,841 --> 00:31:18,480
65 hours after they began, the Double Eagle ditched into the sea,

517
00:31:18,481 --> 00:31:20,800
three miles off the coast of Iceland.

518
00:31:20,801 --> 00:31:24,720
Defrosting their resolve, the men decided to try it again.

519
00:31:24,721 --> 00:31:28,840
This time, they would add another aviator, Larry Newman.

520
00:31:28,841 --> 00:31:31,840
The stories he told me about their flight,

521
00:31:31,841 --> 00:31:33,840
They were enlightening.

522
00:31:33,841 --> 00:31:35,840
I don't want to say they were thrilling.

523
00:31:35,841 --> 00:31:37,840
It was more like a horror story.

524
00:31:37,841 --> 00:31:40,840
But I was intrigued with the actual difficulty

525
00:31:40,841 --> 00:31:43,840
in the adventure of long-distance ballooning.

526
00:31:43,841 --> 00:31:46,840
The harsh reality of long-distance balloon flights

527
00:31:46,841 --> 00:31:48,840
across the Atlantic was sobering.

528
00:31:48,841 --> 00:31:52,840
Since 1873, there had been a total of 14 attempts

529
00:31:52,841 --> 00:31:54,840
and five deaths.

530
00:31:54,841 --> 00:31:58,840
The crew of the Double Eagle II had only one guarantee.

531
00:31:58,841 --> 00:32:00,840
I guarantee the balloon they don't pay for it,

532
00:32:00,841 --> 00:32:03,080
and they don't pay for it until I prove a takeoff.

533
00:32:03,180 --> 00:32:05,880
I inflate this plane, bring it into equilibrium,

534
00:32:05,980 --> 00:32:07,380
hold it about six feet in the air,

535
00:32:07,480 --> 00:32:08,840
and let it sit there for a half hour.

536
00:32:08,940 --> 00:32:10,980
And if it has any leak any place,

537
00:32:11,080 --> 00:32:13,720
it will come right down to the ground.

538
00:32:13,820 --> 00:32:15,520
I guarantee there's no leak.

539
00:32:15,620 --> 00:32:19,320
On August 11, 1978, Double Eagle 2,

540
00:32:19,420 --> 00:32:25,000
an 11-story leak-free 160,000 cubic foot nylon balloon

541
00:32:25,100 --> 00:32:27,200
launched from Presque Isle, Maine,

542
00:32:27,300 --> 00:32:30,440
with no other guarantees of survival.

543
00:32:30,441 --> 00:32:33,540
The idea was to surf in front of a high-pressure front,

544
00:32:33,640 --> 00:32:36,540
which would carry the balloon along on a mass of air.

545
00:32:36,640 --> 00:32:39,440
The balloon took off four hours late.

546
00:32:39,540 --> 00:32:41,980
So we missed being in front of it

547
00:32:42,080 --> 00:32:44,160
and rode essentially on the backside,

548
00:32:44,260 --> 00:32:46,260
like a surfer would be on the backside of a wave

549
00:32:46,360 --> 00:32:47,960
instead of in front of the wave.

550
00:32:48,060 --> 00:32:50,260
Not a great place to surf.

551
00:32:50,360 --> 00:32:54,600
As it turned out, we missed the stronger winds

552
00:32:54,700 --> 00:32:57,240
that would have averaged 40 or 50 miles an hour.

553
00:32:57,241 --> 00:33:01,380
and I believe we only moved about 25 miles per hour.

554
00:33:01,381 --> 00:33:02,740
We ought to check also.

555
00:33:02,880 --> 00:33:04,680
Let's go to 100% and see that we got it.

556
00:33:04,820 --> 00:33:07,580
So the stress was extreme,

557
00:33:08,120 --> 00:33:10,820
knowing that any moment we could go in the ocean,

558
00:33:11,240 --> 00:33:13,760
which we almost did at one portion of the flight.

559
00:33:14,320 --> 00:33:17,780
The gondola, which measured 6 1⁄2 feet by 6 feet,

560
00:33:17,880 --> 00:33:19,920
was recycled from double-equal-1

561
00:33:19,921 --> 00:33:22,380
after it was fished out of the sea near Iceland.

562
00:33:23,660 --> 00:33:26,080
We built a sleeping box that was insulated,

563
00:33:26,081 --> 00:33:30,420
which was very fortunate because of the temperatures at night.

564
00:33:30,421 --> 00:33:34,960
And it was filled with essentials for survival.

565
00:33:34,961 --> 00:33:37,500
The one unique thing about ballooning,

566
00:33:37,501 --> 00:33:40,460
compared to any other form of flight,

567
00:33:40,461 --> 00:33:42,360
it's a still environment.

568
00:33:42,361 --> 00:33:45,260
So once the balloon moves up off the ground

569
00:33:45,261 --> 00:33:48,500
and it's now able to drift,

570
00:33:48,501 --> 00:33:49,940
any wind that was on the ground

571
00:33:49,941 --> 00:33:54,280
as you were standing in the gondola is gone.

572
00:33:54,281 --> 00:33:59,280
We've been on 118 hours now, which is about 13 hours

573
00:33:59,281 --> 00:34:04,280
past the longest flight in history, which was at the house.

574
00:34:04,281 --> 00:34:09,280
Because the balloon is empty inside, it acts as an amplifier.

575
00:34:09,281 --> 00:34:13,280
And so from 20,000 feet above the ocean,

576
00:34:13,281 --> 00:34:15,280
you can hear things on the water.

577
00:34:15,281 --> 00:34:19,280
We could actually hear small objects we would drop,

578
00:34:19,281 --> 00:34:23,280
hit the water 20,000 feet below us.

579
00:34:23,281 --> 00:34:29,860
Ben and I had a discussion over the ocean that maybe we would land and there wouldn't be anybody there,

580
00:34:29,940 --> 00:34:36,740
so we'd have to hide the gondola somewhere under some trees and go to a payphone and call and have somebody come and pick us up.

581
00:34:37,860 --> 00:34:43,280
Yes, the Baligal 2, here we have a French TV and we welcome you in the French space.

582
00:34:44,480 --> 00:34:49,660
When we landed, there were approximately 5,000 people showed up within 10 minutes.

583
00:34:49,661 --> 00:34:54,040
Balloonists had been trying to cross the Atlantic for 150 years.

584
00:34:54,041 --> 00:35:01,600
On August 17, 1978, six days and 2,950 miles after launch,

585
00:35:01,601 --> 00:35:05,300
the crew landed in France and in the record books.

586
00:35:05,301 --> 00:35:12,260
In 1981, Larry Newman and Ben Abruzzo went on to successfully cross the Pacific in Double Eagle 5.

587
00:35:12,560 --> 00:35:19,620
That balloon was still the very basic balloon that was flown back in the 1700s.

588
00:35:19,660 --> 00:35:21,180
It was just a gas balloon.

589
00:35:22,040 --> 00:35:24,020
Now there's all these combinations,

590
00:35:24,200 --> 00:35:26,980
rosiers, this, that, pressurization, etc., etc.

591
00:35:27,540 --> 00:35:30,300
The more things we have that are complex,

592
00:35:30,400 --> 00:35:32,420
the more things there are to break down.

593
00:35:33,180 --> 00:35:35,480
And so this was still simple technology.

594
00:35:35,960 --> 00:35:39,400
Simple balloon technology kept Joe Kittinger alive.

595
00:35:40,040 --> 00:35:41,760
As a prisoner of war in Vietnam,

596
00:35:42,420 --> 00:35:45,980
Kittinger survived extended periods of solitary confinement

597
00:35:45,981 --> 00:35:48,120
by taking flights of fancy.

598
00:35:48,121 --> 00:35:51,560
Everybody had their own little ways of entertaining themselves

599
00:35:51,660 --> 00:35:54,000
and keeping their minds active.

600
00:35:54,100 --> 00:35:55,860
And that was mine, was how to fly around the world

601
00:35:55,960 --> 00:35:57,000
in a balloon.

602
00:35:57,100 --> 00:36:00,940
Almost eight years after Ed Yost's record-setting flight,

603
00:36:01,040 --> 00:36:04,500
Kittinger launched a solo attempt to cross the Atlantic.

604
00:36:04,600 --> 00:36:07,080
I made the balloon for 84,000 cubic feet,

605
00:36:07,180 --> 00:36:09,640
quite a bit large, about 50%.

606
00:36:09,740 --> 00:36:13,540
The balloon's size itself was restricted

607
00:36:13,640 --> 00:36:15,480
to going above 22,000 feet.

608
00:36:15,580 --> 00:36:17,040
So that's basically where I flew.

609
00:36:17,041 --> 00:36:20,380
And it was minus 20 or so, degrees below zero.

610
00:36:20,480 --> 00:36:23,580
After flying for three and a half freezing days,

611
00:36:23,680 --> 00:36:26,680
Kittinger landed in Italy, establishing a new distance

612
00:36:26,780 --> 00:36:28,320
record and accomplishing something

613
00:36:28,420 --> 00:36:31,660
that had never been done before, crossing the Atlantic

614
00:36:31,760 --> 00:36:33,760
in a balloon solo.

615
00:36:33,860 --> 00:36:36,260
The reason why you do something like that is just for fun.

616
00:36:36,360 --> 00:36:39,100
If I couldn't, after the fact, get together with Sherry

617
00:36:39,200 --> 00:36:42,040
and have a glass of champagne or get my buddies and thank them,

618
00:36:42,140 --> 00:36:43,140
who'd want to do it?

619
00:36:43,240 --> 00:36:45,940
You do it because of people and association and friends.

620
00:36:45,941 --> 00:36:46,641
and friends.

621
00:36:49,540 --> 00:36:53,880
Today, most balloonists are in it for fun.

622
00:36:53,980 --> 00:36:56,860
With the advent of propane burners and nylon fabrics

623
00:36:56,960 --> 00:37:00,360
in the 60s, hot air ballooning has become more popular than

624
00:37:00,460 --> 00:37:01,360
ever.

625
00:37:01,460 --> 00:37:05,060
In 1964, there were six hot air balloons registered

626
00:37:05,160 --> 00:37:06,520
with the FAA.

627
00:37:06,620 --> 00:37:10,800
Currently, there are more than 8,500.

628
00:37:10,900 --> 00:37:12,900
In Europe, it's more clubby.

629
00:37:12,901 --> 00:37:20,260
The balloons are owned by large groups, 20, 30 member clubs.

630
00:37:20,760 --> 00:37:24,980
But in the United States, most hot air balloons are owned by individuals, and they're flown for fun.

631
00:37:25,560 --> 00:37:31,820
Nothing is more spectacular or fun than the Albuquerque International Balloon Fiesta,

632
00:37:32,220 --> 00:37:34,260
the largest balloon event in the world.

633
00:37:34,820 --> 00:37:41,520
In 1972, there were just 13 balloons and 10,000 spectators in a shopping center parking lot.

634
00:37:41,521 --> 00:37:46,520
Today there are almost 900 balloons and more than a million spectators.

635
00:37:46,521 --> 00:37:51,520
There are several innovative contests for balloonists, including a key grab,

636
00:37:51,521 --> 00:37:58,520
an event in which pilots position their balloons in an effort to grab the keys to a brand new truck.

637
00:37:58,521 --> 00:38:01,520
Oh my God, here goes my truck!

638
00:38:01,521 --> 00:38:05,520
Without exception, the most popular event is the Special Shape Rodeo,

639
00:38:05,521 --> 00:38:09,120
a jaw-dropping, eye-opening, heart-pumping display

640
00:38:09,220 --> 00:38:11,660
of the most colorful and creative crafts

641
00:38:11,760 --> 00:38:13,760
to float through the sky.

642
00:38:13,860 --> 00:38:17,260
It wasn't until 1976 that the conventional balloon

643
00:38:17,360 --> 00:38:18,560
got a facelift.

644
00:38:18,660 --> 00:38:21,040
These big, benevolent bags of air

645
00:38:21,140 --> 00:38:23,380
are an amalgamation of commercialism

646
00:38:23,480 --> 00:38:27,280
and the technological advances in computers.

647
00:38:27,380 --> 00:38:29,980
An enduring icon, the Energizer Bunny,

648
00:38:30,080 --> 00:38:33,380
is also one of the tallest hot air balloons in the world.

649
00:38:33,381 --> 00:38:38,380
160 feet in length, the ears alone stand 60 feet high.

650
00:38:38,480 --> 00:38:40,380
The complex shape of these balloons

651
00:38:40,480 --> 00:38:43,020
make them especially difficult to fly

652
00:38:43,120 --> 00:38:45,960
because they're awkward aerodynamically.

653
00:38:46,060 --> 00:38:48,700
But even that seems to be just another challenge

654
00:38:48,800 --> 00:38:52,500
for the adventure-charged balloonists.

655
00:38:52,600 --> 00:38:55,400
The Albuquerque International Balloon Fiesta

656
00:38:55,500 --> 00:38:59,140
is considered the most photographed event on the planet.

657
00:38:59,240 --> 00:39:03,380
Balloons will return on Modern Marvels.

658
00:39:03,381 --> 00:39:08,380
From the very beginning, balloons have engaged the human imagination.

659
00:39:08,381 --> 00:39:13,380
That fascination has led to some innovative uses of the vehicle.

660
00:39:13,381 --> 00:39:18,380
A painter in France tried to use a balloon as scaffolding.

661
00:39:18,381 --> 00:39:26,380
In Oregon, helium-filled balloons were used to harvest trees in inaccessible areas.

662
00:39:26,381 --> 00:39:33,620
In 1960, the first communications balloon satellite was launched a thousand miles into space.

663
00:39:35,280 --> 00:39:41,720
And in 1966, the first balloon hospital was shipped to the jungles of South Vietnam.

664
00:39:43,320 --> 00:39:50,540
Equipped with expandable operating rooms, the structure could be collapsed and loaded into a helicopter in less than 15 minutes.

665
00:39:50,541 --> 00:39:53,540
Balloons are also used by the National Weather Service,

666
00:39:53,640 --> 00:39:56,120
which flies 80,000 of them each year

667
00:39:56,220 --> 00:39:58,980
to gather up-to-the-minute atmospheric information

668
00:39:59,080 --> 00:40:03,020
on relative humidity, temperature, and pressure.

669
00:40:03,120 --> 00:40:05,520
Approximately 6 feet in diameter,

670
00:40:05,620 --> 00:40:08,920
the balloons fly to an altitude of 100,000 feet

671
00:40:09,020 --> 00:40:10,800
before bursting.

672
00:40:10,900 --> 00:40:14,960
Historically, balloons have never failed to reinvent themselves

673
00:40:15,060 --> 00:40:16,700
or attract attention.

674
00:40:16,800 --> 00:40:19,300
Their uncanny resilience is due in part

675
00:40:19,301 --> 00:40:21,340
in part to their simplicity.

676
00:40:21,341 --> 00:40:25,000
The advantage of the balloon is that it's quite simple.

677
00:40:25,001 --> 00:40:27,580
It doesn't involve complex moving parts.

678
00:40:27,581 --> 00:40:31,640
We use almost every kind of balloon design

679
00:40:31,641 --> 00:40:33,740
that has been conceived to date,

680
00:40:33,741 --> 00:40:35,920
and we've invented a few besides.

681
00:40:35,921 --> 00:40:39,380
NASA began sending up unmanned high-altitude balloons

682
00:40:39,381 --> 00:40:42,800
in the 70s when scientists started looking at alternatives

683
00:40:42,801 --> 00:40:46,100
to get their experiments into space.

684
00:40:46,101 --> 00:40:50,400
space on rockets and satellites was premium,

685
00:40:50,401 --> 00:40:52,080
and so they're looking for other vehicles.

686
00:40:52,081 --> 00:40:54,840
And balloons end up being a real viable way

687
00:40:54,841 --> 00:40:56,540
to do good science.

688
00:40:56,541 --> 00:41:00,780
The cost is just a fraction of what it would cost

689
00:41:00,781 --> 00:41:02,420
to put a rocket up.

690
00:41:02,421 --> 00:41:05,160
The average stratospheric polyethylene balloon

691
00:41:05,161 --> 00:41:09,960
is 40 million cubic feet, or about 20 acres of plastic.

692
00:41:09,961 --> 00:41:12,520
They are capable of carrying scientific payloads

693
00:41:12,521 --> 00:41:18,060
of up to 8,000 pounds and can fly up to 26 miles above the Earth.

694
00:41:18,061 --> 00:41:21,500
On takeoff, they could be as tall as the Washington Monument.

695
00:41:21,501 --> 00:41:24,340
And once they reach float altitude and the gas inside

696
00:41:24,341 --> 00:41:29,680
expands, the balloon is as wide as two 747 jumbo jets,

697
00:41:29,681 --> 00:41:31,860
nose to tail.

698
00:41:31,861 --> 00:41:35,140
Flights last from a couple of days to a couple of weeks.

699
00:41:35,141 --> 00:41:39,640
But NASA plans to change all that.

700
00:41:39,641 --> 00:41:41,120
We've got new materials these days,

701
00:41:41,320 --> 00:41:45,240
and they instituted a new program called ultra-long duration ballooning.

702
00:41:45,660 --> 00:41:49,340
The goal of that program is to provide a balloon system

703
00:41:49,341 --> 00:41:53,860
that will take 2,000 pounds of science up to 120,000 feet

704
00:41:53,861 --> 00:41:55,360
and put it there for 100 days.

705
00:41:55,900 --> 00:41:58,080
While ultra-long duration balloon flights

706
00:41:58,081 --> 00:42:00,120
will be commonplace in the new century,

707
00:42:00,600 --> 00:42:04,640
new materials will also allow balloons to fly into space.

708
00:42:05,480 --> 00:42:08,300
The new space balloons are made of plastic

709
00:42:08,301 --> 00:42:10,480
that is 10 times stronger than steel

710
00:42:10,481 --> 00:42:14,340
and can tolerate temperatures of 600 degrees Fahrenheit.

711
00:42:14,980 --> 00:42:19,660
Today, we're now seeing that balloons can be used in planetary research

712
00:42:19,661 --> 00:42:24,160
and in exploring the seven other planets in the solar system that have atmospheres.

713
00:42:24,700 --> 00:42:28,380
NASA plans to launch its first interplanetary aerobot,

714
00:42:28,540 --> 00:42:31,580
or robotic balloon, by rocket in 2003.

715
00:42:32,640 --> 00:42:36,240
Now, what you're seeing here is the spacecraft approaching Mars.

716
00:42:36,241 --> 00:42:40,560
The probe now floats down towards the surface of Mars,

717
00:42:41,000 --> 00:42:45,160
and right about now, the balloon will be deployed.

718
00:42:47,220 --> 00:42:53,240
And you can see the balloon here taking pictures of the surface.

719
00:42:54,220 --> 00:42:57,080
It will last for up to about three months in the atmosphere,

720
00:42:57,820 --> 00:43:02,940
and it'll float in the prevailing winds across the surface at a few miles high.

721
00:43:02,941 --> 00:43:10,640
Unlike their earthly predecessors, interplanetary balloons and payloads are measured in pounds, not tons.

722
00:43:11,380 --> 00:43:15,220
The gondola on the Mars balloon weighs just four and a half pounds.

723
00:43:15,221 --> 00:43:22,260
The real advantage of the balloon is that it's a thousand times closer than an orbiter, an orbiter in space.

724
00:43:23,880 --> 00:43:30,380
But it's a thousand times higher above the surface than a rover, and so it has a much greater perspective.

725
00:43:30,381 --> 00:43:33,080
Every idea comes round again.

726
00:43:33,081 --> 00:43:35,780
There are very few ideas in the world,

727
00:43:35,781 --> 00:43:38,080
and certainly this is the case here,

728
00:43:38,081 --> 00:43:40,380
but I think what you have to really look at here is,

729
00:43:40,381 --> 00:43:42,380
why is this happening now?

730
00:43:42,381 --> 00:43:44,880
On the one hand, the need to explore.

731
00:43:44,881 --> 00:43:48,380
The fact we're exhausting the potential of what we can do

732
00:43:48,381 --> 00:43:51,380
with orbital vehicles, with rovers.

733
00:43:51,381 --> 00:43:55,380
We have to have a new way of exploring the planets.

734
00:43:55,381 --> 00:43:57,380
At the same time, technology is helping us

735
00:43:57,381 --> 00:44:00,380
with the miniaturization of electronics and systems.

736
00:44:00,381 --> 00:44:03,120
It means that very small balloons are feasible.

737
00:44:03,220 --> 00:44:05,260
And the fact that the revolution in materials

738
00:44:05,360 --> 00:44:09,060
is allowing us to build very lightweight balloon envelopes

739
00:44:09,160 --> 00:44:12,960
that can carry comparatively large payload fractures.

